--- PROPOSED extension to DR-0053 SS13/SS14: `unwrap` narrowed by TYPE, and the
--- plaintext it yields CONFINED to the region that opened it.
---
--- DR-0053 SS13 already makes whip a store of data it cannot read, and
--- credential-wrap-carriage.maude proves the two properties that boundary
--- needs: the envelope carries the label, and AEAD binding refuses a
--- cross-context unwrap. What SS13 deliberately does NOT claim is anything
--- about the CONSUMER. `unwrap` returns plaintext to whip as ordinary governed
--- data, and SS14 classes `unwrap` as non-narrowable -- named bare, granting
--- the whole operation -- because no natural glob exists for it.
---
--- The proposal modelled here is that a natural narrowing axis does exist, and
--- it is the TYPE of the wrapped payload rather than a glob over its address:
---
---   grant unwrap PHIKey for PatientRecord to acme::Clinician
---
--- plus a confinement region, so the plaintext exists only inside the scope
--- that named the type:
---
---   open body into PatientRecord with phi_key -> rec { ... }
---
--- The whole question is whether that is a real distinction or decoration on
--- SS13, because SS13's label carriage plus the sticky boundary ALREADY stop
--- unwrapped plaintext reaching an under-cleared sink. Three theorems separate
--- them, and none follows from carriage:
---
---   T1  TYPE NARROWING. A grant issued for one type never opens an envelope
---       of another type under the same credential. One key wraps many types;
---       a possession-based grant opens all of them.
---
---   T2  DURABLE SINKS ARE CLOSED INSIDE THE REGION, REGARDLESS OF CLEARANCE.
---       This is the one that does not follow from IFC, and it is a stronger
---       refusal than the sticky boundary gives: the fact base is dual-gated,
---       so a single-tenant deployment that clears it for `secret` lets
---       today's design put unwrapped plaintext straight into
---       facts.value_json -- dissolving the storage property that motivated
---       wrapping. Clearance is not the question; durability is.
---
---   T3  PERSISTENCE CONFINEMENT, the consequence T2 exists to buy. No
---       plaintext derived from a sealed value is ever persisted at its
---       sealed label. Re-sealing or a granted declassify are the only routes
---       to durability.
---
--- Two modelling commitments make those honest rather than assumed.
---
--- FIRST, every route to a sink funnels through ONE delivery marker, and
--- persistence is derived from the sink's durability rather than from which
--- construct addressed it. An earlier cut of this model had a separate
--- `record` route, and T3 held on that route while the generic sink route
--- reached the same fact base unchecked -- a No-solution that looked stronger
--- than it was. Anything that reaches a durable sink persists, by
--- construction, so a future hole is caught by the same theorem.
---
--- SECOND, the vacuity trap is explicit: a region with no exit satisfies T3
--- trivially. Both legal exits are modelled and both carry a Solution search
--- -- `declassify` into a bounded type (the granted, audited hole) and
--- `reseal` back to ciphertext. The theorems are only interesting because the
--- region demonstrably is not a dead end.
---
--- Grounding:
---   custody(H)              the custodian holds the wrapping key
---   sealedVal(V, L, T, H)   ciphertext carrying label AND type AND handle
---   grantUnwrap(H, T)       PROPOSED: the type-narrowed grant
---   openReq(V, T)           `open V into T`
---   inRegion(V, L, T)       plaintext bound INSIDE the confinement region
---   holds(V, L)             plaintext in whip's ordinary value space
---   delivered(V, S, L)      plaintext reached sink S at label L
---   persistedPlain(V, L)    plaintext reached a DURABLE sink at L
---   persistedCipher(V)      an envelope reached a durable sink -- always fine
mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-CREDENTIAL-UNWRAP-CONFINEMENT is
  protecting BOOL .
  sorts Val Label Ty Hnd Sink Cfg .

  ops v1 v2 : -> Val [ctor] .
  ops secret public : -> Label [ctor] .
  ops tPatient tBilling : -> Ty [ctor] .
  op h1 : -> Hnd [ctor] .
  ops reviewer mailout factbase : -> Sink [ctor] .

  op none : -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op __ : Cfg Cfg -> Cfg [ctor assoc comm id: none] .

  --- Confidentiality dominance: canRead(sink label, data label). Low data may
  --- reach a high sink; high data may not reach a low one.
  op canRead : Label Label -> Bool .
  eq canRead(secret, secret) = true .
  eq canRead(secret, public) = true .
  eq canRead(public, public) = true .
  eq canRead(public, secret) = false .

  --- Durability is a property of the sink, INDEPENDENT of its clearance. The
  --- fact base here is both durable and secret-cleared, which is the whole
  --- adversarial setting: IFC is satisfied and the storage property is not.
  op isDurable : Sink -> Bool .
  --- EVERY sink is durable (DR-0074 SS4: "There is no such thing as a
  --- non-durable sink" -- reaching anything outside whip means creating an
  --- effect, and every effect records its input durably). An earlier cut of
  --- this fixture predated SS4 and made `reviewer` and `mailout` non-durable,
  --- which let in-region plaintext reach a model provider. That described a
  --- system this record does not specify.
  eq isDurable(factbase) = true .
  eq isDurable(reviewer) = true .
  eq isDurable(mailout) = true .

  op custody : Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op holds : Val Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sealedVal : Val Label Ty Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op grantUnwrap : Hnd Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op grantDeclassify : Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op openReq : Val Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op inRegion : Val Label Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op refused : Val -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sinkLabel : Sink Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sinkReq : Val Sink -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op delivered : Val Sink Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op deliveredCipher : Val Sink -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op deriveReq : Val Val -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op declassifyReq : Val Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op resealReq : Val Ty Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op persistedPlain : Val Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op persistedCipher : Val -> Cfg [ctor] .

  vars V W : Val .  vars L LS : Label .  vars T T2 T3 : Ty .
  var H : Hnd .  var S : Sink .

  eq holds(V, L) holds(V, L) = holds(V, L) .
  eq sealedVal(V, L, T, H) sealedVal(V, L, T, H) = sealedVal(V, L, T, H) .
  eq inRegion(V, L, T) inRegion(V, L, T) = inRegion(V, L, T) .
  eq refused(V) refused(V) = refused(V) .
  eq delivered(V, S, L) delivered(V, S, L) = delivered(V, S, L) .
  eq deliveredCipher(V, S) deliveredCipher(V, S) = deliveredCipher(V, S) .
  eq persistedPlain(V, L) persistedPlain(V, L) = persistedPlain(V, L) .
  eq persistedCipher(V) persistedCipher(V) = persistedCipher(V) .

  --- T1's rule. Opening requires custody AND a grant that names THIS type.
  --- The request must also name it, so the region's `into T` is the ceiling.
  crl [open] : sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrap(H, T3) custody(H)
    => sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrap(H, T3) custody(H)
       inRegion(V, L, T)
    if T == T2 and T == T3 .

  --- A grant for another type REFUSES. It does not fall back to opening.
  crl [open-type-mismatch] : sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrap(H, T3) custody(H)
    => sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrap(H, T3) custody(H)
       refused(V)
    if T =/= T3 .

  --- Work inside the region derives further values. They inherit BOTH the
  --- label and the confinement -- a derived field of a patient record is
  --- still in the region, which is what makes T3 bite on v2 as well as v1.
  rl [derive] : inRegion(V, L, T) deriveReq(V, W)
    => inRegion(V, L, T) deriveReq(V, W) inRegion(W, L, T) .

  --- T2's rule. A sink is closed to in-region plaintext however well cleared
  --- it is. This is the refusal the proposal adds over SS13, and the reason
  --- `reseal` exists.
  ---
  --- There is no delivering counterpart, and that is the SS4 correction. The
  --- rule that used to sit here delivered to a CLEARED NON-DURABLE sink; SS4
  --- removes that category, so in-region plaintext reaches no sink at all and
  --- the only routes out are the two exits below.
  crl [sink-in-region-durable] : inRegion(V, L, T) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS)
    => inRegion(V, L, T) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS) refused(V)
    if isDurable(S) .

  crl [sink-in-region-under-cleared] : inRegion(V, L, T) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS)
    => inRegion(V, L, T) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS) refused(V)
    if canRead(LS, L) =/= true .

  --- Ordinary held data -- what a granted declassify produces -- reaches any
  --- cleared sink, durable or not. Nothing about confinement restricts it.
  crl [sink-held] : holds(V, L) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS)
    => holds(V, L) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS) delivered(V, S, L)
    if canRead(LS, L) .

  --- Ciphertext reaches any sink. It is opaque, so no clearance question
  --- arises -- this is what "a store of data it cannot read" means.
  rl [sink-cipher] : sealedVal(V, L, T, H) sinkReq(V, S)
    => sealedVal(V, L, T, H) sinkReq(V, S) deliveredCipher(V, S) .

  --- PERSISTENCE IS DERIVED FROM DURABILITY, NOT FROM THE CONSTRUCT. Every
  --- route to a durable sink funnels through here, so T3 cannot be satisfied
  --- by one route while another stands open.
  crl [persist-plain] : delivered(V, S, L)
    => delivered(V, S, L) persistedPlain(V, L)
    if isDurable(S) .

  crl [persist-cipher] : deliveredCipher(V, S)
    => deliveredCipher(V, S) persistedCipher(V)
    if isDurable(S) .

  --- LEGAL EXIT 1: a granted, audited declassify into a bounded type. The
  --- result leaves the region as ordinary public data.
  crl [declassify] : inRegion(V, L, T) declassifyReq(V, T2) grantDeclassify(T3)
    => inRegion(V, L, T) declassifyReq(V, T2) grantDeclassify(T3) holds(V, public)
    if T == T2 and T == T3 .

  --- LEGAL EXIT 2: re-seal back to ciphertext. This is how in-region work
  --- becomes durable without ever persisting plaintext.
  rl [reseal] : inRegion(V, L, T) resealReq(V, T, H) custody(H)
    => inRegion(V, L, T) resealReq(V, T, H) custody(H) sealedVal(V, L, T, H) .
endm

--- NEGATIVE FIXTURE. The design DR-0053 SS14 has today: `unwrap` is
--- non-narrowable, so the grant is POSSESSION-BASED -- holding it opens any
--- envelope under that credential -- and what it yields is ordinary
--- `holds(V, L)` with no region and no scope.
---
--- Note what is NOT wrong here. The label is carried faithfully (SS13 holds),
--- and the sink rule is the same one, so under-cleared sinks still refuse.
--- This fixture is well-behaved by every property carriage claims. It fails
--- exactly the three the confinement proposal adds, which is the point: if it
--- could not be made to fail, the proposal would be decoration.
mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-CREDENTIAL-UNWRAP-CONFINEMENT-POSSESSION is
  protecting BOOL .
  sorts Val Label Ty Hnd Sink Cfg .

  ops v1 v2 : -> Val [ctor] .
  ops secret public : -> Label [ctor] .
  ops tPatient tBilling : -> Ty [ctor] .
  op h1 : -> Hnd [ctor] .
  ops reviewer mailout factbase : -> Sink [ctor] .

  op none : -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op __ : Cfg Cfg -> Cfg [ctor assoc comm id: none] .

  op canRead : Label Label -> Bool .
  eq canRead(secret, secret) = true .
  eq canRead(secret, public) = true .
  eq canRead(public, public) = true .
  eq canRead(public, secret) = false .

  op isDurable : Sink -> Bool .
  --- EVERY sink is durable (DR-0074 SS4: "There is no such thing as a
  --- non-durable sink" -- reaching anything outside whip means creating an
  --- effect, and every effect records its input durably). An earlier cut of
  --- this fixture predated SS4 and made `reviewer` and `mailout` non-durable,
  --- which let in-region plaintext reach a model provider. That described a
  --- system this record does not specify.
  eq isDurable(factbase) = true .
  eq isDurable(reviewer) = true .
  eq isDurable(mailout) = true .

  op custody : Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op holds : Val Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sealedVal : Val Label Ty Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op grantUnwrapAny : Hnd -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op openReq : Val Ty -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sinkLabel : Sink Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op sinkReq : Val Sink -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op delivered : Val Sink Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op deriveReq : Val Val -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op persistedPlain : Val Label -> Cfg [ctor] .

  vars V W : Val .  vars L LS : Label .  vars T T2 : Ty .
  var H : Hnd .  var S : Sink .

  eq holds(V, L) holds(V, L) = holds(V, L) .
  eq delivered(V, S, L) delivered(V, S, L) = delivered(V, S, L) .
  eq persistedPlain(V, L) persistedPlain(V, L) = persistedPlain(V, L) .

  --- THE REJECTED GRANT: possession opens anything under the credential, at
  --- any type, and yields unscoped plaintext.
  rl [open-any] : sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrapAny(H) custody(H)
    => sealedVal(V, L, T, H) openReq(V, T2) grantUnwrapAny(H) custody(H)
       holds(V, L) .

  rl [derive] : holds(V, L) deriveReq(V, W)
    => holds(V, L) deriveReq(V, W) holds(W, L) .

  --- The SAME sink rule as the confined module, and the same clearance check.
  --- Nothing here is more permissive about labels; the difference is only
  --- that there is no region, so durability is never consulted.
  crl [sink-held] : holds(V, L) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS)
    => holds(V, L) sinkReq(V, S) sinkLabel(S, LS) delivered(V, S, L)
    if canRead(LS, L) .

  crl [persist-plain] : delivered(V, S, L)
    => delivered(V, S, L) persistedPlain(V, L)
    if isDurable(S) .
endm
